body:
Any `fetch` call inside a `useEffect` hook must use `AbortController` with a `setTimeout` timeout (e.g. 10s). Without it, if the server doesn't respond, `loading` state persists forever with no user feedback. Pattern:

```typescript
const controller = new AbortController()
const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10_000)
try {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal })
  clearTimeout(timeoutId)
  // ...
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof Error && err.name === 'AbortError') {
    setError('Request timed out. Please try again.')
  }
}
```

**Why:** Issue #1822 was the same bug in a different file (InlineExerciseEditor). This is a recurring pattern - every fetch in useEffect needs this protection.
